    "evidence_text": "Inferring nucleosome positions with their histone mark annotation from ChIP data\n\nMOTIVATION The nucleosome is the basic repeating unit of chromatin.\n\nDNA methylation status predicts cell type-specific enhancer activity.\n\nMechanisms that create and maintain these sites are not well understood.\n\nHuman DNA methylomes at base resolution show widespread epigenomic differences\n\nFunctions of DNA methylation: islands, start sites, gene bodies and beyond\n\nDNA-binding factors shape the mouse methylome at distal regulatory regions\n\nLMRs are occupied by DNA-binding factors and their binding is necessary and sufficient to create LMRs.\n\nClustered methylation occurs at unprecedented densities and directly disfavors nucleosomes, contributing to nucleosome positioning between clusters.\n\nSpecies with linker methylation have small, transcriptionally active nuclei that approach the physical limits of chromatin compaction.\n\nActivator Control of Nucleosome Occupancy in Activation and Repression of Transcription\n\nThe relationship between chromatin structure and gene expression is a subject of intense study.\n\nThe reverse process, repression of transcription, has often been correlated with the presence of nucleosomes.\n\nBut it is not known whether nucleosomes are required for that effect.\n\nThese findings were made possible by our nucleosome occupancy assay.",
